findings.mitigation:
Never let git write the user's working tree. Compute MERGED with merge-tree as approved, then apply each path with a verifying writer:
(1) write the new bytes (from `git cat-file --filters --path=P <blob>`) to a temp file in .git/cowork/tmp on the same volume;
(2) atomically rename the current file to .git/cowork/tmp/<n>.old;
(3) compare the moved file's clean blob (`hash-object --path=P`) with the expected base blob;
(4) create the new file with fs.link(temp, P), which fails with EEXIST if someone recreated P in the meantime, then unlink temp.
On a mismatch or EEXIST, treat the found bytes as a local edit: run a 3-way `git merge-file --object-id` (base=expected, ours=found, theirs=incoming) and retry. If that conflicts, put the found bytes back and record a conflict. Where hard links are unsupported, fall back to rename-over and log the residual window.

findings.test:
Repeat the e1 harness with FileShare.Read and FileShare.None on one of 20 changed paths. Assert:
- the other 19 land;
- the locked path lands within one cycle after release;
- no published tree ever contains the inverse of an incoming hunk;
- outbound publishing continues while the lock is held.

findings.scenario:
Teammates' live work exists in each working tree only as uncommitted changes. Alice:
- runs `git checkout feature`, which carries the changes over;
- or runs `git stash` to test something;
- or uses VS Code Source Control 'Discard Changes' on a file she doesn't recognise, since peers' edits look like her own;
- or runs `reset --hard` or `git pull`.
The daemon pauses only while index.lock exists. The lock is released when the command finishes, and the next cycle snapshots the result against the last-applied tree.

The snapshot runs `git add -A` in a private index, so every non-ignored untracked file is included:
- vim .swp files and its 4913 probe file;
- Emacs #file#;
- JetBrains temp files;
- Excel ~$owner files (hidden attribute is ignored);
- .vs/ state;
- *.orig;
- a local .env;
- outputs the running app writes into the repo (trace logs, test xlsx, build output not in .gitignore).

A macOS or Linux peer (or their Claude) creates any of:
- a path longer than 260 characters (deep test fixtures);
- aux.js, or a name with a trailing dot or space or one of :*?"<>|;
- an NFD-normalised Hangul filename.
The Windows peer's apply runs with core.longpaths=false (the default).

findings.test:
Simulate a human save on a claimed symbol. Assert:
- the holder's next Edit is denied with the diff, and allowed after a Read;
- with overlapping lines there are no markers on disk and both versions are retrievable;
- the human's touched symbols appear as claims, and a peer Claude Edit there is denied.

findings.evidence_detail:
Node one-liner run in <scratchpad>/sweep/ed:
- cp949 bytes 27c7d1b1db270d0a became 27efbfbdd1b1efbfbd270d0a after a utf8 string round-trip, so 'ѱ' appears;
- new TextDecoder() turned efbbbf610a into 610a, dropping the BOM;
- TextDecoder({fatal:true}) throws on the cp949 bytes.

findings.mitigation:
Every conversion between disk and blob goes through git with --path:
- `hash-object -w --stdin --path`;
- `cat-file --filters --path` or `checkout-index --temp`;
- `git merge-file --object-id` for byte merges.
This also respects working-tree-encoding attributes. JavaScript decodes text only for read-only analysis, using {fatal:true, ignoreBOM:true}. A file that fails to decode falls back to whole-file or blank-line-block claims, and the daemon never rewrites it from a string.

findings.scenario:
These all save code mid-thought:
- VS Code autosave afterDelay;
- onFocusChange, which fires when Alice switches to the terminal where her Claude runs;
- JetBrains save-on-frame-deactivation.
The daemon publishes within seconds. Peer Claudes run tests or builds and see a SyntaxError in Alice's function.

Windows 11 setup encourages backing up Desktop and Documents to OneDrive, and repos often live on the Desktop. OneDrive then:
- uploads every daemon write;
- locks files during upload;
- turns files into cloud-only placeholders;
- writes conflict copies such as 'hub-DESKTOP-AB12.py' when two PCs touch the same file.
